<script module>
  import Demo from "$ui/components/Demo.abide"
  import GetReadDemo from "$ui/demos/rpc/reads/GetReadDemo.abide"
  import HeadPingDemo from "$ui/demos/rpc/reads/HeadPingDemo.abide"
  import AwaitReadDemo from "$ui/demos/rpc/reads/AwaitReadDemo.abide"
  import PeekReadDemo from "$ui/demos/rpc/reads/PeekReadDemo.abide"
  import AwaitBlockDemo from "$ui/demos/rpc/reads/AwaitBlockDemo.abide"
  import AwaitInlineDemo from "$ui/demos/rpc/reads/AwaitInlineDemo.abide"
  import RawDemo from "$ui/demos/rpc/reads/RawDemo.abide"
</script>

<main class="page">
  <h1>Reads</h1>
  <p class="lead">A read is <code>GET</code> or <code>HEAD</code>: args go in the URL, the result is cached and coalesced by the <a href="/memo">memo</a> underneath. Define it once, call it by name on either side. This page is the call, then the four ways to render it, then the escape hatch.</p>

  <h2>The read</h2>
  <p>Import the RPC and call it. On the server that is an in-process call; in the browser it is a fetch of the same name — and the read blocks SSR, so the value below is already in this HTML before any JavaScript runs.</p>
  <Demo id="get" title="Read — <code>GET</code>" server="src/server/rpc/rpcGreet.ts" client="src/ui/demos/rpc/reads/GetReadDemo.abide" tab="server">
    <GetReadDemo />
  </Demo>

  <p><code>HEAD</code> is the same read with no body — headers only, useful as a liveness ping. There is <strong>no <code>HEAD</code> helper</strong>: HTTP <code>HEAD</code> <em>is</em> <code>GET</code> minus the body, so the router derives it from the <code>GET</code> you already declared. The server tab below is an ordinary <code>GET</code>; the raw fetch beside it asks for <code>HEAD</code> and gets a 200 status line with an empty body.</p>
  <Demo id="head" title="Read — <code>HEAD</code>, derived from <code>GET</code>" server="src/server/rpc/rpcPing.ts" client="src/ui/demos/rpc/reads/HeadPingDemo.abide" tab="server">
    <HeadPingDemo />
  </Demo>

  <h2>Rendering it: blocking or not</h2>
  <p>Everything below reads the <em>same</em> RPC. The only question is what you want to happen while it is in flight, and there are two answers — wait, or show what you have.</p>

  <p>Waiting is the default and the one to write: <code>{'{'}await fn(args){'}'}</code> blocks the render, so the value lands in the server HTML — the string below is in this document before any JavaScript runs. Nothing suspends on the client; the text node fills when the read settles.</p>
  <Demo id="await-read" title="<code>{'{'}await fn(){'}'}</code> — blocking read" server="src/server/rpc/hello.ts" client="src/ui/demos/rpc/reads/AwaitReadDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <AwaitReadDemo />
  </Demo>

  <p class="note">The <code>await</code> is not optional. Every expression slot is auto-awaited at runtime, so a bare <code>{'{'}fn(args){'}'}</code> would render the value too — but it types as <code>Promise&lt;T&gt;</code>, which dead-ends the moment you want a field. <code>abide check</code> rejects it and points here, so there is one form, not two.</p>

  <p>Not waiting is <code>peek</code>: a reactive <code>T | undefined</code> snapshot that never blocks. It returns whatever the slot holds at that instant and kicks a coalesced load — so it is the value when the slot is already warm (on the server too, if an earlier read of the same call settled during that render), and <code>undefined</code> when it is cold. Reach for it when a slow read shouldn't hold up the paint, and when you want to compose an expression around that <code>undefined</code> rather than have the framework hide it: it is an ordinary value, so the placeholder is ordinary too — <code>{'{'}fn.peek(args) ?? 'Loading…'{'}'}</code>.</p>
  <Demo id="peek-read" title="<code>{'{'}fn.peek(args){'}'}</code> — non-blocking snapshot" server="src/server/rpc/hello.ts" client="src/ui/demos/rpc/reads/PeekReadDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <PeekReadDemo />
  </Demo>

  <h2>Rendering it: branching on the outcome</h2>
  <p>A read can also fail, and <code>{'{'}#await{'}'}</code> is where that becomes visible: three branches for the three states, so a pending message and an error path are structural rather than something you hand-roll.</p>
  <Demo id="await-block" title="<code>{'{'}#await{'}'}</code> / <code>{'{'}:then{'}'}</code> / <code>{'{'}:catch{'}'}</code>" server="src/server/rpc/rpcGreet.ts" client="src/ui/demos/rpc/reads/AwaitBlockDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <AwaitBlockDemo />
  </Demo>

  <p>When you don't need a pending branch, the <code>then</code> binding folds into the opener. Same blocking behaviour as <code>{'{'}await fn(){'}'}</code>, but it binds the resolved value — the compact form for "I just want the value, typed".</p>
  <Demo id="await-inline" title="<code>{'{'}#await fn() then v{'}'}</code> — inline blocking form" server="src/server/rpc/rpcGreet.ts" client="src/ui/demos/rpc/reads/AwaitInlineDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <AwaitInlineDemo />
  </Demo>

  <h2>Stepping outside the memo</h2>
  <p>All of the above go through the memo — cached, coalesced, throwing on failure. <code>fn.raw(args, init?)</code> is the door out: the untouched <code>Response</code>, no caching and nothing thrown, so you read the status, headers, and body yourself. See what the response helpers put on the wire on <a href="/rpc/responses#raw">Responses</a>.</p>
  <Demo id="raw" title="Raw bypass — untouched <code>Response</code>" server="src/server/rpc/rpcGreet.ts" client="src/ui/demos/rpc/reads/RawDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <RawDemo />
  </Demo>

  <p class="note">Beyond <code>peek</code>, a read carries the full reactive probe surface — <code>pending</code>, <code>refreshing</code>, <code>error</code>, <code>watch</code> — because it is a memo. They have their own page: <a href="/rpc/probes">RPC probes</a>.</p>
</main>
